    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Wettability is recognized as an important factor that influences fluid flow and fluid retention behavior in porous reservoir rock. The results of capillary pressure measurements are influenced by the wettability of the rock surface. Wettability of flat mineral surfaces can be determined by measurement of contact angles. Both capillary pressure curves and contact angle measurements are known to be affected by temperature. In this study both types of measurements were made at elevated temperatures using the same fluid pairs and the same rock/mineral surfaces.The porous diaphragm method was used in equipment similar to 22 23 that described earlier. ' The fluid pairs were distilled water plus Chevron white oil No. 15 in one case, and a solution of Eastman Kodak naphthenic acids in this white oil in the other case. The porous media were ignited Berea sandstone, fluxed Berea sandstone, ignited Boise 22 sandstone and a limestone sample used earlier. Fused silica and calcite crystals were used for the contact angle measurements.Temperature level had an effect on the shape and location of the capillary pressure curves for both systems. The solution of naphthenic acids in white oil caused a change towards a more oil wet medium, which was expected because of the polar nature of the naphthenic acids. Limestone was affected appreciably by the presence of naphthenic acids and by temperature level, whereas the effect of naphthenic acids on sandstone was not as great. Results with white oil showed a decrease in the hysteresis between drainage and imbibition capillary pressure curves at elevated temperatures. However, runs with naphthenic acids in white oil exhibited an increasing hysteresis at elevated temperatures, which indicates more oil is left in the pores due to increased oil wetness of cores.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
